# Carrier Wound #22: A Re-Drive Collides With Its Own Orphaned Lane Owner

Wound #21 (btoa) was fixed, tested, committed (`da1b04d`) and deployed. The
re-drive it gated cleared the Latin1 ceiling — and then blocked terminal again,
on a **different** wound. No "btoa" in the blocker this time; the planner agent
still never ran:

```
[09:04:56Z] phase=None term=False dg=1 stale=True  activeLanes=1 dispatches=0 nodes=0 blk=None
[09:05:37Z] phase=None term=True  dg=2 stale=False activeLanes=1 dispatches=0 nodes=0 blk=adapter_unavailable
```

```json
"blocker": {
  "code": "adapter_unavailable",
  "message": "Agent lane admission deferred: lane:planner:run-live-20260614T085001608Z-91baf146 (already-active); retry after 1s."
}
```

Generation 2's events: `planningDynamicWorkflow` at `09:05:37.326` → `blocked`
at `09:05:37.395`. **69 milliseconds.** The planner is a ~213s sandbox agent;
69ms means it never spun up. It died on admission — colliding with a lane owner
**its own gen-1 drive left behind.** This is the sixth wound the live-observe
mandate caught from the outside-invisible / node-by-node-obvious gap.

## Mechanism

Three layers (Layer A reclaims the self-owned slot; Layer B self-bounds the
planner's pi-invoke; Layer C makes the whole-script reaper *announce its cause*).
Layers A and B are proven by live evidence; Layer C is the wound the **post-B
re-drive** surfaced — and it is the reason that re-drive still died causeless.

**Layer A — admission can't reclaim its own orphan (PROVEN, fixed here).** The
zombie-budget pre-admit (wound #16) reserves a lane's OWNER slot in
`activeLaneOwners[laneId]` *before* dispatch, so a torn drive can be reaped. But
a drive torn *after* that reservation and *before* it persists a lane release
leaves the slot held with no live process behind it. Lane ids are run-scoped —
`lane:planner:<runId>` — so the owner can **only ever be the same run.** Yet
`admitLaneCore` blindly deferred any active laneId:

```ts
if (activeLaneIds.includes(input.laneId)) {
  return { status: "deferred", reason: "already-active", retryAfterSeconds: 1, ... };
}
```

`admitted-agent-lane-runtime.ts` throws on `deferred`; `workflow-app.ts`'s
catch-all forges that throw into terminal `adapter_unavailable`. So the run's
next drive asks to run its planner, collides with the ghost of its previous
drive's planner, and **gets deferred against itself — permanently.** The
generation fence + stall-generation reaper guarantee the gen-1 drive is already
dead (`dg=1 stale=true` then `dg=2`) before gen-2's admission runs, so the slot
is provably reclaimable. The deferral was never legitimate contention; for
run-scoped ids it is **always** self-collision.

**Layer B — gen-1's synchronous planner hung at the pi-invoke step (PROVEN
structural, fixed here).** Gen 1 sat in `planningDynamicWorkflow` from `08:50:55`
until the reaper marked it stale ~13.7 min later — `drivingMarker.stale:true`,
`lastDriveFailure: null` = an uncaught isolate death, not a caught error. Layer
A's deploy turned that blind stall into a **live, attributable read.** A fresh
re-drive carried the step-boundary heartbeat (`/workspace/.piwf-heartbeat`,
written before each step, read before sandbox destroy and re-thrown enriched),
and it named the wedge exactly:

```
adapter_unavailable ... "Command timeout after 180000ms" [step heartbeat:
 1781433411 install-pi-agent | 1781433436 prepare-auth | 1781433436 clone-artifacts |
 1781433439 checkout-branch | 1781433439 mount-packages | 1781433441 pi-invoke]
```

The unix-stamped heartbeat decodes the whole lane: install ran **25s**
(1781433436 − 1781433411 — cold but finite, and *variable*: a colder probe paid
~132s); prepare-auth/clone/checkout/mount were all instant; **pi-invoke started at
1781433441 and was still running when the 180000ms command ceiling killed it at
~1781433591** — pi-invoke is both the longest step (~150s) and the last one
reached. The `180000ms` is not a magic number: it is the decouple applied to the
worker's *then-deployed* `WORKFLOW_APP_TIMEOUT_MS` of `300000`
(`laneExecTimeoutMs = 300000 − 120000`). HEAD carries `600000` (unchanged in git
since spine `0d8a247`), so the redeploy that ships this fix raises the live lane
ceiling to `480000` and pi-invoke's self-bound budget to `480 − elapsed − 30`.
Two structural facts, both proven by the heartbeat, not guessed:

1. **pi-invoke was unbounded.** The decoded lane script calls `pi --provider … -p …`
   directly — no inner `timeout` — so a hung model call (egress black-hole,
   auth-retry storm, runaway generation) rides until the whole-script `timeout`
   SIGTERMs it, and SIGTERM skips the `emit_failure_marker` EXIT trap, so pi's
   stderr is never captured. The blocker carries a heartbeat but **no cause**: a
   slow error wearing a fast error's silence.
2. **The cold install steals a *variable* slice of the budget.** De-dockering
   (task #15) moved the pi-agent `npm install` to runtime; sandboxes are
   per-run/per-lane cold (`buildSandboxId(runId, laneId)`), so every planner pays
   the install (25s here, ~132s on a colder run) before pi even starts. pi-invoke
   inherits only the remainder — which is exactly why the self-bound budget is
   computed from measured `elapsed`, not a fixed slice.

**Carrier invariant confirmed by SDK source** (`@cloudflare/sandbox@0.11.0`): the
default `"http"` transport's request path is a bare
`globalThis.fetch`/`stub.containerFetch` with **no `signal` and no client-side
request timeout** — the 120s `DEFAULT_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS` belongs only to the
unused WebSocket transport. So a sync `sandbox.exec` has exactly one ceiling: the
**server-side command timeout** the SDK forwards as `timeoutMs` (= the lane's
`input.input.timeoutMs`). This is load-bearing for Layer B's fix — it means the
in-script `timeout`s can be aligned to that single ceiling with no hidden
client-side abort racing ahead of the EXIT trap and stranding the receipt.

**Layer C — a SIGTERM'd script can't run an EXIT-only trap, so the reaper kills
the cause (PROVEN by the post-B re-drive).** Layer B's deploy was the instrument,
and the very next live dream re-drive ran it: it sat in `planningDynamicWorkflow`
and then terminated `blocked / adapter_unavailable` with `nodes=0` — **again
causeless,** the blocker carrying no pi stderr at all:

```
[11:25:25Z] status=planningDynamicWorkflow lanes=1 dg=1 nodes=0 disp=0 blk=None
[11:29:48Z] status=blocked            lanes=0 dg=1 nodes=0 disp=0 blk=adapter_unavailable
```

Reading the three nested timeouts that actually wrap the lane explains why
Layer B's receipt-on-exit-124 never reached the caller:

- **OUTER** — `sandbox.exec(wrapped, { timeout: input.input.timeoutMs })` = the
  480000ms server-side command ceiling. On expiry the SDK **throws** "Command
  timeout after 480000ms" and **discards stdout entirely.**
- **MIDDLE** — `timeout "$PIWF_COMMAND_TIMEOUT_SECONDS" bash script` inside
  `wrapCommandForSandbox`, which **SIGTERMs** the script.
- **INNER** — Layer B's `timeout "$pi_budget_s" pi …`, self-bounded to
  `MIDDLE − elapsed − tail_margin`.

Two compounding facts make Layer B's stderr vanish anyway:

1. **MIDDLE == OUTER, so they raced.** `PIWF_COMMAND_TIMEOUT_SECONDS` was
   `ceil(timeoutMs/1000)` = **480s**, the exact wall the OUTER 480000ms throw
   sits on. When they fire together the server-side throw wins, and it **drops
   stdout** — so even a perfectly-emitted result marker is erased before
   `runCommand` can parse it.
2. **A bare SIGTERM never runs an EXIT-only `trap`.** Layer B bounds *pi*, but
   the post-pi **tail** — normalize → transcript → hash → build-receipt →
   `git add/commit/push` → emit-OK-marker — is itself unbounded inside the
   script. When pi self-bounds late (exit 124) the tail can overrun its ~30s
   margin (a slow `git push` to the artifact remote is the obvious culprit), and
   then the **MIDDLE** `timeout` SIGTERMs bash. `trap emit_failure_marker EXIT`
   does **not** fire on a signal-kill — bash dies without emitting anything. The
   blocker names a heartbeat step with **no cause**: the same slow-error silence
   Layer B fixed for *pi*, reopened one layer out in the *tail*.

So the run that was supposed to prove Layer B instead proved that Layer B's
guarantee — "a hang commits a receipt carrying pi's stderr" — only holds if the
tail finishes before the MIDDLE reaper, and the reaper, when it does fire, still
runs the marker. Layer C closes both: it makes the SIGTERM itself emit the
marker, and it pulls MIDDLE strictly below OUTER so the marker survives.

## Fix — Reclaim the Self-Owned Slot, as a Class

`admitLaneCore` now discriminates on owner identity before deferring:

```ts
const currentOwner = record.activeLaneOwners[input.laneId];
if (currentOwner !== undefined) {
  if (currentOwner === input.runId) {
    await this.ensureReaperAlarm();      // keep the reclaimed lane watched
    return { status: "admitted", admissionId, admittedAt: nowIso(), ... };
  }
  return { status: "deferred", reason: "already-active", retryAfterSeconds: 1, ... };
}
```

Three properties:

1. **Same run reclaims.** The owner mapping already names this run, so re-admit
   is idempotent — no second owner is leaked, the active count is invariant.
2. **Reaper stays armed.** `ensureReaperAlarm()` on the reclaim keeps the
   watchdog alive so the re-driven lane is still bounded.
3. **Different run still defers.** The safety branch holds even though
   run-scoped ids make cross-run collision on a single laneId unreachable in
   production. Defensive, not load-bearing.

## Chaos Receipt (would have caught it)

`tests/integration/cloudflare-run-reaper.test.ts` — 12 passing (2 new). The
hostile double reproduces the production sequence against the real supervisor DO
(fake D1 + fake DO storage), not a polite mock:

- **re-admits a run colliding with its own orphaned lane owner**: admit reserves
  the slot → the drive tears (slot persists, no release) → the same run
  re-admits → `admitted`, with exactly one owner still held (no leak).
- **still defers a DIFFERENT run colliding with an active lane owner**: a
  foreign run on the same laneId → `deferred/already-active`, and it neither
  steals nor duplicates ownership.

Before the fix the first test returned `deferred` — the exact production
blocker. A polite double that released the slot on teardown would have masked
the orphan entirely; the test deliberately leaves the slot held.

## Fix Layer B — Self-Bound pi-invoke to the Remaining Budget

The wedge is observability-first, not a guess at *why* pi hangs. pi-invoke now
carries its own inner `timeout`, sized from the time actually left before the
whole-script SIGTERM:

```bash
pi_elapsed_s=$(( $(date +%s) - script_start_s ))
pi_budget_s=$(( PIWF_COMMAND_TIMEOUT_SECONDS - pi_elapsed_s - PIWF_PI_INVOKE_TAIL_MARGIN_SECONDS ))
if [ "$pi_budget_s" -lt 1 ]; then pi_budget_s=1; fi
timeout "$pi_budget_s" pi --provider "$PI_PROVIDER" --model "$PI_MODEL" ... -p "$(cat "$LANE_PROMPT_PATH")" > "$raw_output_path" 2> "$stderr_path"
pi_status=$?
if [ "$pi_status" -eq 124 ]; then
  printf '\n[pi-invoke self-bound] pi exceeded its %ss budget and was terminated by timeout; the heartbeat names pi-invoke as the wedge step.\n' "$pi_budget_s" >> "$stderr_path"
fi
```

Four properties:

1. **pi self-times-out BEFORE the whole-script SIGTERM.** Exit 124 lands inside
   the script while the EXIT trap is still live, so normalize-output →
   build-receipt → git-commit → git-push all run. A hang now **commits a
   receipt** carrying pi's stderr tail instead of vanishing on SIGTERM.
2. **The budget is computed from elapsed time, not a constant.** The variable
   cold-install lead (25s–~132s observed) cannot starve or overrun pi's slice —
   `pi_budget_s = ceiling − elapsed − tail_margin` adapts every run, always
   bounding pi to finish by `ceiling − tail_margin` of script time regardless of
   what the install cost.
3. **On 124 it appends a synthetic stderr line** naming pi-invoke as the wedge,
   so even an empty-stderr hang is self-attributing.
4. **The lane budget is decoupled from the reaper deadline.**
   `cloudflare-worker-route.ts` no longer hands the lane the full
   `WORKFLOW_APP_TIMEOUT_MS` (600000, unchanged in git since the spine commit
   `0d8a247` — there was never a 300000→600000 edit). It now carves a reaper
   margin: `laneExecTimeoutMs = max(60_000, WORKFLOW_APP_TIMEOUT_MS −
   LANE_EXEC_REAPER_MARGIN_MS) = 600000 − 120000 = 480000`. That 480s becomes the
   lane's single command-timeout ceiling; with the 30s
   `PIWF_PI_INVOKE_TAIL_MARGIN_SECONDS`, pi gets `480 − elapsed − 30` (≈ 419s after
   a 25s install, ≈ 312s after a ~132s one) and in either case is bounded to finish
   by ~450s of script time — leaving the post-pi normalize→receipt→commit→push
   steps a fixed 30s window before the 480s ceiling, and the 480s lane ceiling
   itself well inside the 600s reaper deadline so a wedged lane surfaces its
   blocker before the reaper mistakes it for stale. The earlier live wedge died at
   the **180000ms** ceiling the decouple produced from the worker's then-deployed
   `WORKFLOW_APP_TIMEOUT_MS` of `300000` (`300000 − 120000`); the fix is the
   decouple + self-bound on a `600000`-based ceiling, not a raised constant.

This is the Google SRE discipline applied to a sandbox lane —
*Monitoring Distributed Systems*:

> On the other hand, a slow error is even worse than a fast error! Therefore,
> it's important to track error latency, as opposed to just filtering out errors.

The unbounded pi-invoke was exactly a slow error filtered into silence. The
self-bound converts it into a fast, attributable one.

### Chaos Receipt — Layer B (would have caught it)

`tests/integration/cloudflare-sandbox-agent-lanes.test.ts` — 23 passing (2 new):

- **structural pin**: asserts the real `buildPiAgentLaneCommand()` emits the
  budget arithmetic, the `timeout "$pi_budget_s" pi --provider` wrapper, the
  `< 1` clamp, `script_start_s="$(date +%s)"`, and the 124 self-bound line.
- **hostile behavioral double**: slices the *real* pi-invoke block out of
  `buildPiAgentLaneCommand()`, runs it under `bash -c` with a fake `pi` that
  `sleep 20`s, `PIWF_COMMAND_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=3`, tail margin `1`. Asserts pi is
  killed at exit 124, the post-pi steps are *reached* (`POST_PI_REACHED
  pi_status=124`), and the stderr carries `[pi-invoke self-bound]` naming
  pi-invoke. It reproduces prod transport (real budget math, real `timeout`,
  real 124-attribution) and fakes only the model endpoint — and it earned its
  keep immediately, catching a literal backtick inside the `String.raw` template
  that had corrupted the shell command and that `typecheck` + `check` both waved
  through.

## Fix Layer C — Make the Reaper Announce Its Cause

Two coupled edits, one in the script and one in the env that wraps it.

**(1) Trap the signal, not just the exit** (`cloudflare-sandbox-agent-lane-command.ts`):

```sh
on_signal() {
  emit_failure_marker
  exit 143
}
trap on_signal INT TERM
trap emit_failure_marker EXIT   # idempotent backstop; marker_emitted guard no-ops the double-fire
```

The MIDDLE `timeout` reaps with SIGTERM. `on_signal` runs the *same*
`emit_failure_marker` (which captures `tail -c 2000` of pi's stderr, scrubbed of
credentials, plus `failingStep=$current_step`, `piStatus`, `gitLogTail`) and then
`exit 143` (128+15). The `exit` re-triggers the EXIT trap, which the
`marker_emitted=1` guard turns into a no-op — exactly one marker, whether the
script ends by clean exit, by error, or by signal. Before this, a tail that
overran the margin took a SIGTERM straight to `/dev/null`.

**(2) Pull MIDDLE strictly below OUTER** (`cloudflare-sandbox-agent-lanes.ts`):

```ts
const WHOLE_SCRIPT_REAPER_GUARD_SECONDS = 30;
// MIDDLE shell timeout, decoupled BELOW the OUTER server-side ceiling so its
// SIGTERM fires first, runs the marker trap, and the marker reaches stdout
// before the OUTER throw discards it.
PIWF_COMMAND_TIMEOUT_SECONDS: String(
  Math.max(1, Math.floor(input.input.timeoutMs / 1000) - WHOLE_SCRIPT_REAPER_GUARD_SECONDS)
),
```

With the live ceiling at `480000ms`, MIDDLE drops from 480s to **450s**. The
reaper SIGTERM now fires a guaranteed 30s before the OUTER throw, so
`on_signal`'s marker flushes through `bash`'s stdout and `sandbox.exec` *returns*
that stdout — `runCommand` parses the marker, and the existing error-marker path
(`cloudflare-sandbox-agent-lanes.ts` ~331) surfaces `marker.stderrTail` into the
blocker. pi's inner budget rides along: `450 − elapsed − 30`, so pi finishes by
~420s of script time and the tail gets a 30s window before the 450s reaper —
and even if the tail blows that window, edit (1) makes the reaper itself emit the
cause. The slow error is converted to a fast, attributable one at *both* layers.

This is *Release It!* (Nygard), Fail Fast / Transparency:

> When a host gets rebooted in the middle of the night and an application fails
> … nobody is going to know unless the application itself announces the problem.
> … Of course, for the application to announce that it failed …, it first has to
> know that it failed. … A running application can be interrogated for its
> internal state, but a halted one cannot.

A signal-killed lane is a halted application: it can't be interrogated after the
fact, so it has to announce the cause *on the way down*. The EXIT-only trap
assumed the lane always got to exit on its own terms. The reaper's whole job is
to deny it that, so the announcement has to live on the signal path too.

### Chaos Receipt — Layer C (would have caught it)

`tests/integration/cloudflare-sandbox-agent-lanes.test.ts` — 25 passing (2 new):

- **structural pin**: asserts the real `buildPiAgentLaneCommand()` emits
  `on_signal() { emit_failure_marker`, `trap on_signal INT TERM`, `exit 143`,
  *and* keeps `trap emit_failure_marker EXIT` — the signal path and the backstop
  must both exist.
- **hostile behavioral double**: slices the real script from `marker_emitted=0`
  to `mark install-pi-agent`, overrides `stderr_path` with a fixture line
  (`pi: clone https://x:supersecrettoken@…/run.git failed: timeout` plus the
  `[pi-invoke self-bound]` marker), sets `current_step=git-push`, `pi_status=124`,
  then `kill -TERM $$` mid-tail with a trailing `sleep 5; printf TAIL_COMPLETED`
  that must **never** run. Asserts the SIGTERM emitted a `status:"error"` marker
  carrying pi's stderr tail, `failingStep:"git-push"`, `piStatus:124`, with the
  credential scrubbed (`https://x:***@`, never `supersecrettoken`) and
  `TAIL_COMPLETED` absent. macOS lacks GNU `timeout`'s process-group SIGTERM, so
  the test signals the unit-level fact directly (`kill -TERM $$`): *does a TERM
  run the marker?* — which is the exact bash semantic the prod MIDDLE `timeout`
  relies on. Before edit (1) this returned no marker; the EXIT-only trap is
  silent on a signal.

