## Burn-In Gate

Per the Phase 2 D3 acceptance bar, the 10-run count stays at **zero**. All three
layers are now deployed; a fresh dream-shape run must reach `captured`
**observed live on deployed code** before the burn-in restarts. The post-B
re-drive already taught the lesson Layer C encodes: a bounded pi is necessary but
not sufficient — the *tail* and the *reaper* both had to learn to speak. With C,
the next re-drive is the real instrument: the planner phase either **completes to
`captured`**, or it **surfaces a marker** — whether pi self-bounded (124 with its
stderr tail) or the tail itself wedged (the reaper's SIGTERM now names the failing
step and carries pi's stderr regardless). There is no remaining path by which the
blocker is causeless; `adapter_unavailable` with `nodes=0` and an empty stderr is
no longer reachable. Whatever the next run blocks on — if it blocks — will *say
why*, which is the whole point. Wound #22 is the recovery-class sibling of the
[[polite-fakes-franchise-wound]]: the carrier could reach `captured` once, but
could not **recover** a torn drive to `captured`. Following
[[carrier-wound-btoa-latin1-ceiling-2026-06-14]], both surfaced only because the
run was watched node-by-node, not stared at.

## Layer C.2 — The Budget Was Mis-Sized For The Whole Operation (probe)

Layer C shipped. The next observed-live re-drive,
`run-live-20260614T120131363Z-fe5634ee`, did exactly what C promised: it did
**not** block causelessly. It blocked, and the cause was legible:

```
[12:01:46Z] status=planningDynamicWorkflow lanes=1 dg=1 nodes=0 blk=None
[12:09:19Z] status=blocked            lanes=0 dg=1 nodes=0 blk=adapter_unavailable
[12:09:19Z] === TERMINAL ===
```

~453s wall (`12:01:46`→`12:09:19`). That is not a hang — it is the OLD
`WORKFLOW_APP_TIMEOUT_MS=600000` derivation's shell reaper firing:
`laneExecTimeoutMs = max(60_000, 600_000 − 120_000) = 480_000`;
`PIWF_COMMAND_TIMEOUT_SECONDS = floor(480_000/1000) − 30 = 450`. The 453s
observed ≈ the 450s shell reaper + a few seconds of tail. `proposePlan` threw on
the killed lane, and `workflow-app.ts:1430` wraps **any** planner throw as
`adapter_unavailable` — so the short code lied about the class, but the new
`selectLaneAbortDiagnostic` payload now rides inside `error.message` and will
carry pi's own stderr on the next blocker detail instead of the masked clone log.

The heartbeat decomposition (Layer B's instrument) read the lane from the
inside: **137s** cold `npm install -g` of the pi agent (`install-pi-agent` →
`prepare-auth`), then **285s** of genuine pi runtime ending at exit **124**
(`pi-invoke` → `normalize-output`, self-bound). pi's budget was
`450 − 137 − 30 = 283s`; it used all 285s of real work and the reaper took the
tail. **Slow-but-real, not a hang.**

The root cause is a budget that no longer accounts for the whole operation.
De-docker (#15, `131bfe8`) moved the pi-agent install **out** of the baked image
and **into** the lane at runtime — but `WORKFLOW_APP_TIMEOUT_MS` stayed at
`600000`, sized for a world where the lane only ran pi. The variable cold-install
tax (25–137s) now eats 30% of the planner's budget before pi gets a token.

> Building Microservices, 2nd ed. (Sam Newman), Ch. 12 — Retries:
> "having a budget for how long an operation is allowed to take can be a useful
> idea… If you are going to retry, you need to take this into account when
> considering your time-out threshold."

The install is not a retry, but the lesson is the same shape: the **overall**
budget has to cover every actor that holds the clock — install **and** pi **and**
the commit/push tail — not just the headline work. The budget was set for one
actor and three moved in.

**Probe (this commit), honestly labelled:** `WORKFLOW_APP_TIMEOUT_MS`
`600000 → 900000`. Re-derived: `laneExecTimeoutMs → 780_000`;
`PIWF_COMMAND_TIMEOUT_SECONDS → 750`; planner pi budget → `750 − 137 − 30 ≈ 583s`
(2× the 283s it had), absorbing the worst-case install tax with tail headroom.
The reaper deadline moves to 15 min. This is a **mitigation + a correctly-resized
budget**, not the final class fix. It is also a **high-information probe**: a
single sync `sandbox.exec` now asked to hold a 780s ceiling will either (a) carry
the planner to a real plan and let the rest of the pipeline run — teaching us the
budget was the only wall — or (b) hit a Cloudflare request/DO wall below 780s,
which is itself the proof that the sync planner cannot hold the budget and forces
the reserved class fix.

**Reserved class fixes (NOT yet applied):**
1. **Wire the planner to the async dispatch/poll/receipt path** (FIX B, already
   built + tested for worker lanes — `dispatchStep`/`pollStep`/`readStepReceipt`,
   multi-alarm budget). The planner adapter `proposePlan` is the lone sync
   holdout on a single held `sandbox.exec` ceiling. This is the durable answer if
   the probe hits a platform wall.
2. **Pre-baked registry image** carrying the pi agent, killing the runtime
   install tax at the source (the inverse of #15's de-docker, but via a published
   image instead of a local `./Dockerfile` that re-introduces the macOS keychain
   deploy hell).

### Observability fix shipped alongside the probe

`selectLaneAbortDiagnostic` (in the pure `cloudflare-sandbox-agent-lane-command.ts`,
so the test graph never drags in the Cloudflare SDK). The old blocker used
`gitLogTail || stderrTail` — which **masked** pi's stderr behind a benign
"Cloning into… empty repository…" line exactly when pi had run and self-bound.
The selector keys on `piStatus`: pi ran (number) → lead with **stderr**, append
the git log as context; pi never ran (null, a pre-pi clone/checkout failure) →
lead with the **git log**. The application announces WHICH actor halted, not just
THAT one did. Three unit tests pin it (pi self-bound 124 leads with
`[pi-invoke self-bound]` + appends `[git log: …]`; pi-never-ran leads with the
clone failure; empty/empty → "no diagnostic output").

